  "cut_point": "We are given:\n\n- $ S = \\{ m \\mid m \\in \\mathbb{N}_{+}, \\text{ m's prime factors are all less than or equal to 10} \\} $\n- We are to find the **smallest positive integer $ n $** such that **any subset of size $ n $** of $ S $ will **contain 4 distinct numbers whose product is a perfect square**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the set $ S $\n\nWe are told that the prime factors of a number $ m $ in $ S $ **must be all less than or equal to 10**.\n\nThis means $ m $ can have prime factors like 2, 3, 5, 7. Numbers in $ S $ are **square-free** (no common factors other than 1) with at most one prime factor in their decomposition, or multiple, but all prime factors are ≤ 10.",
